## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why this breaks

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. `ThreadAbortException` is never raised, and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006`).

---

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Cleanup on cancellation | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue to loop condition check
                // Loop exits naturally because token.IsCancellationRequested is true
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts == null || _workerThread == null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signals cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for graceful shutdown
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't stop — it's stuck in uncooperative code.
            // Log a warning. Cannot force-kill in modern .NET.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| **`GetNextItem()` blocks forever** — cancellation can't interrupt it | Refactor to accept `CancellationToken`. If it wraps a `BlockingCollection`, use `Take(token)`. If it's a `WaitHandle`, use `WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { handle, token.WaitHandle })`. |
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Cooperative cancellation only works at checkpoints. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive operations inside `ProcessItem`. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Always dispose via `IDisposable` or `finally`. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions. |
| **Replacing `Thread.Sleep` in similar code** | Use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` — it returns immediately when cancelled. `Thread.Sleep` ignores the token. |

---

### If `GetNextItem` cannot be modified

If it calls uncooperative third-party/native code that truly blocks without accepting a token, your only option is to move that work to a **child process** and call `Process.Kill()` on timeout. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET.
